---
import Layout from '../../layouts/Layout.astro'
import { ogPathFor } from '../../data/og'
import PromptCard from '../../components/PromptCard.astro'
import ShellSwitch from '../../components/ShellSwitch.astro'
import SideQuest from '../../components/SideQuest.astro'

// One title everywhere (taste review r2, 2026-11-06): the post's own headline.
// "Grow an agent" is the workshop's name; it lives in the eyebrow.
const title = "Just give the model a keyboard | Ouro"
const description = "Build an agent loop from an empty folder in four small steps, with your coding agent writing the code. Learn what an agent loop is and how agents actually work, then hand your agent the keyboard and watch it grow."
const canonicalUrl = "https://ouroboros.bot/blog/build-ai-agent-from-scratch/"
const datePublished = "2026-03-01"
const dateModified = "2026-11-11"

const yt = "https://youtube.com/watch?v=YFjfBk8HI5o&t=9298"
const transcript = "https://lexfridman.com/peter-steinberger-transcript#how-openclaw-works"

// The commands a learner types. Setup is the same in every shell, one line at
// a time, because Windows PowerShell 5 has no &&.
const setup = "mkdir agent\ncd agent\nnpm init -y\nnpm pkg set type=module\nnpm install openai"
const exportsUnix = "export GITHUB_TOKEN=$(gh auth token)\nexport COPILOT_API=$(gh api /copilot_internal/user --jq .endpoints.api)"
const exportsPs = "$env:GITHUB_TOKEN = gh auth token\n$env:COPILOT_API = gh api /copilot_internal/user --jq .endpoints.api"
const preflightUnix = "curl -O https://ouroboros.bot/agent-loop/preflight.mjs\nnode preflight.mjs"
const preflightPs = "Invoke-WebRequest https://ouroboros.bot/agent-loop/preflight.mjs -OutFile preflight.mjs\nnode preflight.mjs"
const quotaUnix = "unset GH_TOKEN\nexport GITHUB_TOKEN=$(gh auth token --user <name>)"
const quotaPs = "Remove-Item Env:GH_TOKEN\n$env:GITHUB_TOKEN = gh auth token --user <name>"

const promptM1 = `We're building a tiny agent in TypeScript, one milestone per file: m1.ts, m2.ts, m3.ts and m4.ts. Each file stands alone so I can run any of them.

This folder already has a package.json with "type": "module" and the openai package installed. Use only the openai package: no agent SDK, no other dependencies. Create the client with new OpenAI({ apiKey: process.env.GITHUB_TOKEN, baseURL: process.env.COPILOT_API }) and use the model claude-sonnet-5.5.

I'll run each file with node, for example node m1.ts. Node runs .ts files by stripping the types, so stick to erasable TypeScript (no enums), with no build step and no tsconfig.

Now write m1.ts: send one chat completions request with a single user message, "What is the secret word in secret.txt?", and print the reply. Claude can split one reply across several choices, so join the text from every item in response.choices, not just the first. Keep it short.`

const promptM2 = `Write m2.ts: a chat in the terminal, with the same client and model as m1.ts.

Keep a messages array. Read a line from me with node:readline, using the prompt "you> ". Push it as a user message, send the whole array, and print the reply as "agent> " followed by the text. Join the text across all choices, like m1.ts. Push the reply as an assistant message, then ask me again. Skip empty lines. I'll leave with Ctrl+C.`

const promptM3 = `Write m3.ts: one request, like m1.ts, asking "What is the secret word in secret.txt?". This time, pass a tools array with one tool: read_file, described as "Read a text file and return its contents.", taking one string argument, path.

Join the text across all choices, and collect tool_calls from all choices too, not just the first. For each tool call, print "The model asked for a tool call:" and the raw call as pretty JSON. Then really read the file and print what you got.

Don't send the result back to the model. Stop there and print "...but we never sent the result back, so the model never answers. That's milestone 4."`

const promptM4 = `Write m4.ts: the chat from m2.ts, plus tools, in a loop.

Tools: read_file from m3.ts; write_file, taking path and content, described as "Write text to a file, replacing it if it exists."; and shell, taking one command string. Run shell commands with powershell.exe -NoProfile -NonInteractive -Command on Windows and /bin/sh -c everywhere else, and name that shell in the tool's description so the model writes the right syntax. On Windows, start each command with try { [Console]::OutputEncoding = [Text.Encoding]::UTF8 } catch {}; so the output comes back as UTF-8, with accents and emoji intact.

After each of my messages, call the model. Join the text and collect tool_calls across all choices. Push the assistant message, with its tool_calls. If there are no tool calls, print the text and wait for me. Otherwise run each tool, print a line like "  [read_file] {"path":"secret.txt"}", push each result as a tool message with its tool_call_id, and call the model again.

Keep it safe: at most 20 tool rounds per turn; a 30-second timeout on shell commands; tool output capped at 10 KB; JSON.parse of the arguments guarded, so bad JSON comes back to the model as an error string; unknown tools answered with an error string; and a non-zero exit code reported to the model along with the output.

Add a --show-messages flag that prints the messages array as JSON after each turn.`

const promptName = `Read m4.ts. That's your own source code. Pick a name for yourself, then change the "agent> " label to it. Tell me when to restart.`

const growList = `Add a list_directory tool to yourself. Read m4.ts, follow the pattern of the tools already there, add the definition and the handler, and write the file. Then tell me how to test it.`

const growStream = `Make yourself nicer to use. Stream your replies: pass stream: true and print the text as it arrives, rebuilding the text and tool_calls from the deltas of every choice. Show a small spinner on stderr until the first chunk arrives, with no new dependencies. Catch API errors, print them clearly and go back to the prompt instead of crashing. Check that GITHUB_TOKEN and COPILOT_API are set before you start.`

const growRegistry = `Your tools are getting crowded. Refactor them into a registry: one object that maps each tool name to its definition and its handler. Running a tool should just look it up. Keep everything else working the same.`

const growPurpose = `Add a system message at the start of messages. Before you write it, ask me questions, one per turn, about who you should be and how I like to work. Make sure you know you're a coding agent with file and shell tools, and that m4.ts is your own code. Introduce yourself when you start.`

const growSkills = `Build a skills system. Make a skills folder with two example skills as markdown files, code-review.md and explain.md. Add a list_skills tool, and a load_skill tool that reads a skill and adds it to your system message.`

const growToolmaker = `Write a skill called toolmaker.md that teaches you how to add a tool to yourself: the definition, the handler, and a reminder to tell me to restart. Then load it and use it to add a get_current_time tool.`

const toolCallSample = `<span class="text-mist">The model asked for a tool call:</span>
<span class="text-shadow">{</span>
  <span class="text-bone">"id"</span><span class="text-shadow">:</span> <span class="text-scale">"…"</span><span class="text-shadow">,</span>
  <span class="text-bone">"type"</span><span class="text-shadow">:</span> <span class="text-scale">"function"</span><span class="text-shadow">,</span>
  <span class="text-bone">"function"</span><span class="text-shadow">: {</span>
    <span class="text-bone">"name"</span><span class="text-shadow">:</span> <span class="text-scale">"read_file"</span><span class="text-shadow">,</span>
    <span class="text-bone">"arguments"</span><span class="text-shadow">:</span> <span class="text-scale">"{\\"path\\":\\"secret.txt\\"}"</span>
  <span class="text-shadow">}</span>
<span class="text-shadow">}</span>

<span class="text-mist">We ran read_file("secret.txt") and got:</span>
<span class="text-scale">pineapple</span>
<span class="text-mist">...but we never sent the result back, so the model never answers. That's milestone 4.</span>`

const loopCode = `<span class="text-mist">for</span> <span class="text-shadow">(</span><span class="text-mist">let</span> <span class="text-bone">round</span> <span class="text-shadow">=</span> <span class="text-scale">1</span><span class="text-shadow">;</span> <span class="text-bone">round</span> <span class="text-shadow">&lt;=</span> <span class="text-scale">20</span><span class="text-shadow">;</span> <span class="text-bone">round</span><span class="text-shadow">++) {</span>
  <span class="text-mist">const</span> <span class="text-bone">reply</span> <span class="text-shadow">=</span> <span class="text-mist">await</span> <span class="text-bone">askModel</span><span class="text-shadow">(</span><span class="text-bone">messages</span><span class="text-shadow">,</span> <span class="text-bone">tools</span><span class="text-shadow">)</span>
  <span class="text-bone">messages</span><span class="text-shadow">.</span><span class="text-bone">push</span><span class="text-shadow">(</span><span class="text-bone">reply</span><span class="text-shadow">)</span>
  <span class="text-mist">if</span> <span class="text-shadow">(!</span><span class="text-bone">reply</span><span class="text-shadow">.</span><span class="text-bone">tool_calls</span><span class="text-shadow">)</span> <span class="text-mist">return</span>   <span class="text-shadow">// no tool calls: it's done</span>
  <span class="text-mist">for</span> <span class="text-shadow">(</span><span class="text-mist">const</span> <span class="text-bone">call</span> <span class="text-mist">of</span> <span class="text-bone">reply</span><span class="text-shadow">.</span><span class="text-bone">tool_calls</span><span class="text-shadow">) {</span>
    <span class="text-bone">messages</span><span class="text-shadow">.</span><span class="text-bone">push</span><span class="text-shadow">(</span><span class="text-bone">runTool</span><span class="text-shadow">(</span><span class="text-bone">call</span><span class="text-shadow">))</span>   <span class="text-shadow">// hand back the result</span>
  <span class="text-shadow">}</span>
<span class="text-shadow">}</span>`

const termSkills = `<span class="text-scale">you&gt;</span> <span class="text-bone">list skills</span>
<span class="text-scale">you&gt;</span> <span class="text-bone">load code-review, then review m4.ts</span>`

const faq = [
  {
    q: "What is an agent loop?",
    a: "An agent loop is the cycle that makes an AI agent work: the model reads the conversation, decides on an action (like reading a file or running a command), your program runs it and hands back the result, and the model decides again. It repeats until the model answers without asking for anything. The loop turns a model that can only talk into an agent that can act.",
  },
  {
    q: "How do AI agents work?",
    a: "An AI agent is a language model with tools, inside a loop. The model can't run anything itself; it asks for a tool call, the program runs it and adds the result to the conversation, and the model goes again. Three tools, read file, write file and run shell, let an agent do almost anything a developer can do at a terminal, including change its own code.",
  },
  {
    q: "How many lines of code does it take to build an AI agent?",
    a: "The loop itself fits on one screen. The finished milestone 4 file in this guide, with three tools and safety limits, is about 90 lines of TypeScript. Streaming, memory, skills and everything else grow from there, often written by the agent itself.",
  },
  {
    q: "What tools does an AI agent need?",
    a: "At minimum, three: read file, write file and run a shell command. With file access and a shell, a model can install packages, run tests, call APIs and change code. You can add more later, and the agent can add them to itself.",
  },
]

---

<Layout title={title} description={description} ogImage={ogPathFor('/blog/build-ai-agent-from-scratch')} canonical={canonicalUrl} ogType="article">

  <Fragment slot="head">
    <meta property="article:published_time" content={datePublished} />
    <meta property="article:modified_time" content={dateModified} />
    <meta property="article:author" content="Ari Mendelow" />
    <meta property="article:tag" content="AI agents" />
    <meta property="article:tag" content="agent loop" />
    <meta property="article:tag" content="TypeScript" />
    <meta name="author" content="Ari Mendelow" />
    <script type="application/ld+json" set:html={JSON.stringify({
      "@context": "https://schema.org",
      "@type": "TechArticle",
      "headline": "Just give the model a keyboard",
      "description": description,
      "author": {
        "@type": "Person",
        "name": "Ari Mendelow",
        "url": "https://github.com/arimendelow"
      },
      "publisher": {
        "@type": "Organization",
        "name": "Ouro Labs",
        "url": "https://ouroboros.bot",
        "logo": {
          "@type": "ImageObject",
          "url": "https://ouroboros.bot/images/ouroboros.png"
        }
      },
      "datePublished": datePublished,
      "dateModified": dateModified,
      "mainEntityOfPage": canonicalUrl,
      "keywords": ["AI agent", "agent loop", "how to build an AI agent", "what is an agent loop", "how do AI agents work", "TypeScript agent", "LLM agent", "agentic AI", "workshop"],
      "inLanguage": "en",
      "isAccessibleForFree": true
    })} />
    <script type="application/ld+json" set:html={JSON.stringify({
      "@context": "https://schema.org",
      "@type": "FAQPage",
      "mainEntity": faq.map(({ q, a }) => ({
        "@type": "Question",
        "name": q,
        "acceptedAnswer": { "@type": "Answer", "text": a }
      }))
    })} />
  </Fragment>

  <article class="px-6 lg:px-16 pt-32 pb-20">
      <!-- Header -->
      <header class="max-w-6xl mb-12 md:mb-8 mx-auto split lg:items-end">
        <div class="split-head">
          <span class="text-xs font-mono text-fang uppercase tracking-[0.22em]">Grow an agent</span>
          <h1 class="font-display text-4xl md:text-5xl lg:text-6xl text-bone leading-tight mt-4">
            Just give the model a keyboard
          </h1>
        </div>
        <div class="split-body">
          <p class="text-base md:text-lg text-mist mt-5 leading-relaxed">
            Build an agent loop from an empty folder in four small steps. Then hand it the keyboard and watch it grow.
          </p>
          <div class="flex items-center gap-4 mt-6 text-sm text-mist">
            <span>Ari Mendelow</span>
            <span class="text-canopy">·</span>
            <span>November 2026</span>
          </div>
        </div>
      </header>

    <div class="max-w-6xl mx-auto">

      <div class="prose-ouro">

        <p><strong class="text-bone">The loop fits on one screen, and it isn't the clever part.</strong></p>
        <p>
          Every coding agent you've used, the ones that read your files, run your tests and fix their own mistakes,
          sits on a loop short enough to read in one breath. The model is the clever part. The loop just keeps handing
          it the keyboard.
        </p>
        <p>
          Lex Fridman once reminded Peter Steinberger, who built OpenClaw, that he'd said everybody should implement an
          agent loop at some point in their lives. <a href={yt}>Peter agreed</a>:
        </p>
        <blockquote>It's like the Hello World in AI, you know? And it's actually quite simple.</blockquote>
        <p>
          (There's a <a href={transcript}>transcript</a> too.) He's right on both counts. Today you write that Hello
          World. Within the hour you'll have an agent that reads files, runs commands and edits its own code,
          and you'll have watched every piece of it arrive.
        </p>

        <h2>Why take the long way</h2>
        <p>
          Most days I don't give my agents steps. I give them the outcome I want and trust them with the rest. That's
          <a href="/approach/">how I work</a>, and <a href="/blog/trust-the-agent/">why it works</a>. You could do that
          here too: tell any coding agent "make me an agent loop" and you'd have one in a minute.
        </p>
        <p>
          You'd also have a file you don't understand. So today we take the long way, one small step at a time, until
          the loop stops being magic and becomes yours. A coding agent still writes every line. You steer.
        </p>

        <h2 id="before-you-start">Before you start</h2>
        <p>You'll need:</p>
        <ul>
          <li>Node 22.18 or newer. It runs TypeScript files directly, so there's no build step.</li>
          <li>The GitHub CLI, <code>gh</code>, signed in to an account with GitHub Copilot.</li>
          <li>A coding agent you like: Copilot CLI, Claude Code, Codex, any of them.</li>
          <li>One terminal you'll keep open the whole way through.</li>
        </ul>
        <p>
          Make a fresh folder for your agent and give it the one package it needs. Pick your shell, then type these
          one line at a time:
        </p>
        <ShellSwitch mac={setup} powershell={setup} />
        <p>
          A fresh folder matters. Soon your agent will run any command it likes in here, so keep nothing secret in it.
          If <code>npm init</code> gives you trouble, save <a href="/agent-loop/package.json">package.json</a> into
          the folder instead and run <code>npm install</code>.
        </p>
        <p>
          For the model, borrow the GitHub Copilot access you already have. These two lines hand your token and
          Copilot's address to everything you run in this terminal:
        </p>
        <ShellSwitch mac={exportsUnix} powershell={exportsPs} />
        <p>
          These Copilot endpoints are undocumented, and every call counts against your Copilot quota. On WSL, install
          <code>gh</code> and sign in inside WSL too; it doesn't share your Windows sign-in. Open a new terminal later
          and you'll need these two lines again.
        </p>
        <p>
          Now prove a real request gets through. <a href="/agent-loop/preflight.mjs">preflight.mjs</a> checks your
          Node version, the package, your token and your account, then asks the model to say hello:
        </p>
        <ShellSwitch mac={preflightUnix} powershell={preflightPs} />
        <p>When it prints "All set", you're ready. If it doesn't, it says what to try, and so does this table.</p>

        <div id="troubleshooting" class="trouble my-6" role="region" aria-label="If something goes wrong" tabindex="0">
          <table>
            <thead>
              <tr><th scope="col">You see</th><th scope="col">Try</th></tr>
            </thead>
            <tbody>
              <tr>
                <td>An error that mentions <code>OPENAI_API_KEY</code></td>
                <td>Your token isn't set in this terminal. Run the two lines above again, in the terminal you run your agent from.</td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <td><code>401</code></td>
                <td>Your sign-in has lapsed. Run <code>gh auth login</code>, then the two lines again.</td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <td><code>403</code></td>
                <td>This account has no Copilot. Switch to one that does with <code>gh auth switch</code>, then run the two lines above again so your token follows. Or name the account directly with the <a href="#quota-fix">two lines under this table</a>.</td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <td><code>400</code>, model not supported</td>
                <td>Your plan doesn't offer that model. Use <code>gpt-4.1</code> instead.</td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <td><code>400</code>, not accessible through chat completions</td>
                <td>That model only speaks the newer Responses API. Pick another model, or take the <a href="#responses-api">Responses side quest</a>.</td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <td><code>429</code>, quota exceeded</td>
                <td>That account has used its Copilot quota. Point your token at an account with quota, using the <a href="#quota-fix">two lines under this table</a>.</td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <td>Still the wrong account after switching</td>
                <td>Run the two lines above again. If that doesn't help, a <code>GH_TOKEN</code> variable is overriding <code>gh</code>. The <a href="#quota-fix">two lines under this table</a> clear it and name the account.</td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <td><code>gh: command not found</code></td>
                <td>Install the GitHub CLI and run <code>gh auth login</code>. WSL needs its own.</td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <td>PowerShell rejects <code>&amp;&amp;</code></td>
                <td>Windows PowerShell has no <code>&amp;&amp;</code>. Run the commands one line at a time.</td>
              </tr>
            </tbody>
          </table>
        </div>

        <p id="quota-fix">
          Out of quota, or on the wrong account? <code>gh auth status</code> lists the accounts you're signed in to.
          Replace <code>&lt;name&gt;</code> with the one you want, then run both lines:
        </p>
        <ShellSwitch mac={quotaUnix} powershell={quotaPs} />
        <p>
          Leave <code>&lt;name&gt;</code> in and the line fails (PowerShell won't even parse it), so do swap it. If PowerShell says
          it can't find <code>GH_TOKEN</code>, that's fine: there was nothing to clear.
        </p>

        <aside id="byok-signpost" class="post-note my-8">
          <p class="!mb-0 !text-base">
            <strong class="text-bone">Side quest: bring your own key.</strong> Rather use MiniMax, Azure OpenAI or
            OpenAI? <a href="#bring-your-own-key">Swap two variables and a model name.</a>
          </p>
        </aside>

        <h2>Plant a secret</h2>
        <p>Your agent needs something to find. Hide a word in a file. Pick any word you like:</p>
        <ShellSwitch mac="echo pineapple > secret.txt" powershell="Set-Content secret.txt pineapple" />
        <p>Remember it. In a minute you'll ask the model for it, and it won't have a clue.</p>

        <h2 id="m1">Milestone 1: first contact</h2>
        <p>
          Start with the smallest thing that could work: send the model one message and print what comes back. This
          first prompt also tells your coding agent how the whole project fits together, so the next three go quickly.
        </p>
        <div class="my-6"><PromptCard label="Tell your coding agent" prompt={promptM1} /></div>
        <p id="m1-try">Try it:</p>
        <ShellSwitch mac="node m1.ts" powershell="node m1.ts" />
        <p>
          Behind, or want to check your work? Save <a href="/agent-loop/m1.ts">m1.ts</a> into your folder and run it.
          Every milestone has one.
        </p>
        <p>
          The model answers, politely and confidently, and without your word. It might guess. It might apologize. It
          can't know: it has never seen your folder. All it has is the one message you sent. (If its reply opens with a
          <code>&lt;think&gt;</code> block, <a href="#think-tags">there's a side quest for that</a>.)
        </p>

        <h2 id="m2">Milestone 2: conversation</h2>
        <p>
          The model has no memory. Every request starts from nothing, so a chat is just your program keeping a list of
          messages and sending the whole list every time. That list is called <code>messages</code>. Keep an eye on it.
        </p>
        <div class="my-6"><PromptCard label="Tell your coding agent" prompt={promptM2} /></div>
        <p>Try it:</p>
        <ShellSwitch mac="node m2.ts" powershell="node m2.ts" />
        <p>Catch up with <a href="/agent-loop/m2.ts">m2.ts</a>.</p>
        <p>
          Tell it your name, then ask what your name is. It remembers, because you sent it back. Ask for the secret
          word, and it still has no idea. Press Ctrl+C when you're done.
        </p>

        <h2 id="m3">Milestone 3: one tool</h2>
        <p>
          Time to give the model its first key. A tool is just a description: a name, a sentence about what it does,
          and the shape of its arguments. The model can't run anything. It can only ask. Here's what asking looks like.
        </p>
        <div class="my-6"><PromptCard label="Tell your coding agent" prompt={promptM3} /></div>
        <p>Try it:</p>
        <ShellSwitch mac="node m3.ts" powershell="node m3.ts" />
        <p>Catch up with <a href="/agent-loop/m3.ts">m3.ts</a>. You'll see something like this:</p>
        <div class="rounded-xl border border-canopy/60 bg-deep/80 overflow-hidden my-6">
          <div class="flex items-center justify-between gap-4 pl-4 pr-2 py-2.5 border-b border-canopy/40 bg-undergrowth/50">
            <span class="font-mono text-xs text-shadow">output</span>
          </div>
          <pre class="p-5 text-sm font-mono leading-relaxed overflow-x-auto" tabindex="0" data-copy-exempt="program output, shown as evidence"><code set:html={toolCallSample} /></pre>
        </div>
        <p>
          Look at that. The model knew it needed the file, named it, and asked for it in tidy JSON. Your program read
          it, and the word is right there on your screen. The model will never see it, because nobody told it.
        </p>
        <p><strong class="text-bone">It asked, and nobody answered.</strong></p>

        <h2 id="m4">Milestone 4: the loop</h2>
        <p>
          So answer it. Run the tool, add the result to <code>messages</code>, and call the model again. If it asks for
          another tool, do it again. When it stops asking, it's done. While you're there, give it two more keys: one to
          write files and one to run commands. Read, write and run are enough to do nearly anything you can do at a
          terminal.
        </p>
        <div class="my-6"><PromptCard label="Tell your coding agent" prompt={promptM4} /></div>
        <p>Try it, and ask for the secret word:</p>
        <ShellSwitch mac="node m4.ts" powershell="node m4.ts" />
        <p>Catch up with <a href="/agent-loop/m4.ts">m4.ts</a>.</p>
        <p>
          This time it reaches for <code>read_file</code>, gets the word back, and tells you. Then ask for something you
          never planned: "write a haiku to hello.txt", "how many lines is m4.ts?", "what's in this folder?". It reads,
          writes, runs, checks its own work and answers.
        </p>
        <p>Now run it once more with the curtain open:</p>
        <ShellSwitch mac="node m4.ts --show-messages" powershell="node m4.ts --show-messages" />
        <p>
          After every turn you'll see <code>messages</code>: your words, its tool calls, the results you handed back,
          its answer. There's no hidden state and no memory anywhere else. The whole agent is that list and this loop:
        </p>
        <div class="rounded-xl border border-canopy/60 bg-deep/80 overflow-hidden my-6">
          <div class="flex items-center justify-between gap-4 pl-4 pr-2 py-2.5 border-b border-canopy/40 bg-undergrowth/50">
            <span class="font-mono text-xs text-shadow">the shape of the loop in m4.ts</span>
          </div>
          <pre class="p-5 text-sm font-mono leading-relaxed overflow-x-auto" tabindex="0" data-copy-exempt="a sketch of the loop to read; your coding agent writes the real file"><code set:html={loopCode} /></pre>
        </div>
        <p><strong class="text-bone">That's it. That's the agent.</strong></p>

        <h2 id="handoff">Hand it the keyboard</h2>
        <p>
          Everything so far, you built through a coding agent. You don't need it now. You have one.
        </p>
        <p>
          Run <code>node m4.ts</code> and talk to yours instead. It can read <code>m4.ts</code>. It can change
          <code>m4.ts</code>. A few minutes ago it couldn't find a word in a file. Start by letting it name itself:
        </p>
        <div class="my-6"><PromptCard label="Tell your agent" prompt={promptName} /></div>
        <p>
          Press Ctrl+C, run it again, and say hello. It just edited its own code. If it ever breaks itself, your coding
          agent is still there, and so is <a href="/agent-loop/m4.ts">m4.ts</a>.
        </p>
        <p>It's real, and it's small. Before you trust it with anything that matters, here's what it doesn't have yet:</p>
        <ul>
          <li><strong>Memory.</strong> Quit, and it forgets everything. <code>messages</code> lives only as long as the process.</li>
          <li><strong>Safety.</strong> It runs any command it likes, in your folder, as you. That's why the folder is fresh.</li>
          <li><strong>Compaction.</strong> Every turn makes <code>messages</code> longer. One day it won't fit in the model's context, and the agent stops working.</li>
          <li><strong>Recovery.</strong> If the network drops or the model errors, it crashes.</li>
        </ul>
        <p>Most of a production agent is answers to those four. The loop stays the same.</p>

        <h2 id="keep-growing">Keep growing</h2>
        <p>From here, every change is a conversation with your agent. Restart it after each one so it runs as its new self.</p>

        <h3>A new tool</h3>
        <div class="my-6"><PromptCard label="Tell your agent" prompt={growList} /></div>
        <p>Restart and ask what's in the folder. It just gave itself a new ability.</p>

        <h3>Nicer to use</h3>
        <p>Replies arrive all at once, and errors crash it. Fix both, and watch the words stream in.</p>
        <div class="my-6"><PromptCard label="Tell your agent" prompt={growStream} /></div>

        <h3>Tidy tools</h3>
        <div class="my-6"><PromptCard label="Tell your agent" prompt={growRegistry} /></div>
        <p>Watch it refactor itself. Restart, and check it still works.</p>

        <h3>A sense of purpose</h3>
        <div class="my-6"><PromptCard label="Tell your agent" prompt={growPurpose} /></div>
        <p>Restart. This is where it stops being a demo and starts being yours.</p>

        <h3>Skills</h3>
        <div class="my-6"><PromptCard label="Tell your agent" prompt={growSkills} /></div>
        <p>Restart, then try:</p>
        <div class="rounded-xl border border-canopy/60 bg-deep/80 overflow-hidden my-6">
          <div class="flex items-center justify-between gap-4 pl-4 pr-2 py-1.5 border-b border-canopy/40 bg-undergrowth/50">
            <span class="font-mono text-xs text-shadow">terminal</span>
            <button type="button" data-copy-target="code-termSkills" class="btn-ghost !py-2 text-sm flex-none"><span data-copy-label>Copy</span></button>
          </div>
          <pre id="code-termSkills" class="p-5 text-sm font-mono leading-relaxed overflow-x-auto" tabindex="0"><code set:html={termSkills} /></pre>
        </div>
        <p>
          Notice what a skill is: a markdown file. Your agent could already read one with <code>read_file</code>. The
          new tools just make the habit easy.
        </p>

        <h3>Skills that build tools</h3>
        <div class="my-6"><PromptCard label="Tell your agent" prompt={growToolmaker} /></div>
        <p>
          A skill that teaches it to make tools, used to make a tool. Each growth makes the next one easier. Keep going:
          a web search tool, a git tool, a skill for writing tests, saving and resuming conversations.
        </p>

        <h2 id="sky">Sky's the limit</h2>
        <p>
          In February I was building a plugin for Slugger, my OpenClaw agent, and the plugin fought me at every turn. I
          wanted to understand the thing underneath, so Slugger and I wrote the first version of this guide. Then I
          followed it myself: I built the loop, about 150 lines of it, handed it the keyboard and kept going.
        </p>
        <p>
          That bootstrap became the Ouroboros harness. It runs <a href="/butler/">Butler</a>, which looks after my home
          server, and Shepherd, which keeps the agents in my terminals on task, every day. It's still where I try new
          harness ideas first. <a href="/desk/">Desk</a> grew out of the same exploration; it's a sibling, and it works
          with whichever harness you use. They're all on the <a href="/apps/">apps page</a>, and the whole path is in
          <a href="/lab/story/">the story</a>. To try the harness itself, start with
          <a href="/lab/docs/getting-started/">getting started</a>.
        </p>
        <p>
          You opened this page with an empty folder and a secret word. You're leaving with something that can change
          itself. Mine became a harness. Yours can become anything.
        </p>

        <h2 id="side-quests">Side quests</h2>

        <SideQuest id="bring-your-own-key" title="Bring your own key" back="byok-signpost">
          <p>
            Your code only needs a key, an address and a model name. Point the same two variables at any provider with
            an OpenAI-style chat completions API, then change the model name in your files. <code>GITHUB_TOKEN</code>
            and <code>COPILOT_API</code> are just labels; the code doesn't care what's in them.
          </p>
          <ul>
            <li><strong>OpenAI:</strong> your OpenAI key, the address <code>https://api.openai.com/v1</code>, and a model like <code>gpt-4.1</code>.</li>
            <li><strong>MiniMax:</strong> your MiniMax key, the address <code>https://api.minimax.io/v1</code>, and a model like <code>MiniMax-M2.5</code>.</li>
            <li><strong>Azure OpenAI:</strong> your resource's key, the address <code>https://your-resource.openai.azure.com/openai/v1/</code>, and your deployment name as the model.</li>
          </ul>
          <p>
            Skip <code>preflight.mjs</code>, which checks your GitHub sign-in; <code>node m1.ts</code> is your check
            instead. Or tell your coding agent which provider you use and let it make the change.
          </p>
        </SideQuest>

        <SideQuest id="responses-api" title="Newer GPT models" back="troubleshooting">
          <p>
            Some newer OpenAI models only answer through the Responses API, and chat completions turns them away with
            a <code>400</code>. The loop is the same; the shapes change. You send a list of input items instead of
            <code>messages</code>, a tool call arrives as a <code>function_call</code> item, and you answer it with a
            <code>function_call_output</code> item carrying the same <code>call_id</code>.
          </p>
          <div class="my-6"><PromptCard label="Tell your coding agent" prompt={"Copy m4.ts to m4-responses.ts and switch it to client.responses.create, with the model I give you. Keep the same tools and safety limits. Keep the conversation as a list of input items, append each response's output items, run every function_call item, and send back a function_call_output item with the matching call_id, until a round has no function calls."} /></div>
        </SideQuest>

        <SideQuest id="think-tags" title="Models that think out loud" back="m1-try">
          <p>
            Some reasoning models put their thinking in the reply itself, between <code>&lt;think&gt;</code> and
            <code>&lt;/think&gt;</code>. It's harmless, just noisy. Ask your coding agent to hide it when printing, or to
            print it dimmed so you can watch the model reason. Some providers want the thinking sent back with the
            conversation, so check their docs before you drop it from <code>messages</code>.
          </p>
        </SideQuest>

        <h2 id="faq">Frequently asked questions</h2>
        {faq.map(({ q, a }) => (
          <>
            <h3>{q}</h3>
            <p>{a}</p>
          </>
        ))}

      </div>

    </div>
  </article>

</Layout>

